bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-32-Every persisted custody carrier and every act that touches it has exactly one target class. ''D'' dissolves confidential material into rows owned by one PersonaGroup and sealed with that group's face custody key (FCK). ''F'' carries deliberately public material or ciphertext a blind floor may copy; it has no custody decryption key. ''S'' carries a *named* node-only confidential row under a named node principal and opener. A shared verb creates no shared key. A mixed carrier splits before it receives a class. The browser has no vessel custody key (S key). A node has no residual omnibus key: only an actual S row can justify a node custody door. Current `hot`/`cold` mean S-sealed, not target ownership (`vault-carriers.ts:5-22,64-68`). These are operator rulings recorded at `stable-founding-road.mem:205-207`.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-33-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:34:The face identifier in this document means a stable, canonical PersonaGroup identifier. Current `h<N>` is a local handle index, not sufficient by itself as a cross-vessel cryptographic identity. ''LEAD:'' migrations map each current handle to one authenticated PersonaGroup before assigning an FCK or moving bytes; an ambiguous map refuses the migration. A row with no provable owner refuses rather than falling into F or S.
