bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-148-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-149-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:150:''LEAD target wire format:'' each D seal authenticates one canonical context containing a format/domain name and version, vessel or store namespace, immutable PersonaGroup ID, row name, row kind, and the complete logical row reference. Encode fields unambiguously as length-prefixed bytes, analogous to the sealed value context at `sealed-store.mem:85-106`. Supply the expected context from the authenticated row selector on open; never trust a ciphertext's claimed face. A carrier copied into another face's slot, a different row name, or a different kind fails authentication. F and S use distinct domain names; an S row binds its named node principal and row kind. New names/formats refuse old ciphertext rather than treating it as clear. ''LEAD:'' exact byte layout and migration version receive implementation review before K15.6; these fields and refusal behavior are normative now.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-151-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-152-Generalize `writeSealedCarrier` from `(key, tree, carrier)` to an explicit `(row class, owner, key, matching tree/door, bound carrier context)`. The caller proves that the key opens the *same owner and row class* against its standing slot tree; an S writer proves its named node door; F has no sealed-carrier writer. Preserve the existing exclusive act, absent-or-authentically-opened standing row rule, and refusal without replacement (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-288`). Seal a full caller-supplied replacement with the same context, write a sidecar, read it back byte-identical, authenticate/open it, then rename (`:290-304`). A failed old-row open, class mismatch, tree mismatch, stale/ambiguous migration shape or failed readback leaves the occupied carrier byte-for-byte unchanged. A whole archive replacement is not an implicit read-modify-write. No encrypt-only capability can perform this overwrite proof. The matching-key rule also governs delete and rotation; never use A's key or node S to authenticate B's standing row.
--
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-159-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-160-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:161:A bundle for PersonaGroup X contains X's root/identity, X's Keyhive and Veil archive, X's selected proof/recovery keys and rows, X's guardian set/commitments, intended wrapped FCK recovery route, X's selected private roster/book lines, and X's content-key memory section and eligible history. It is sealed under the bundle key and its own route (`sealed-store.mem:68`; `vault-carriers.ts:123-165,301-342,377-397`). It never contains a bare FCK, another face's slot or row, another face's identifiers or content keys, a node place signer, a node S key, guardian-held secret shares, or local-only vessel seat/outbox state. `bundle: stays` remains local unless an explicit per-face recovery rule supersedes it. Restore X into a fresh vessel without opening Y, and refuse an ambiguous old mixed archive. Restore does not mint authority over a sibling.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-162-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-163-<<~/ahu>>
--
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-175-# ''Native desktop shell, LEAD:'' keychain/biometric, if used, opens only the selected face's FCK. A second account/session and process restart cannot open A or B without their routes; F remains.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-176-# ''Probe / Mudlet seat:'' with A admitted, request B through the node and by direct local ciphertext open. Both refuse; the probe retains no FCK or node S. ''LEAD:'' probe adapter implementation is outside this carrier census.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:177:# ''Node daemon / hearth:'' boot with no face admission and no omnibus key; F serves. Admit A and run the isolation red over every B node carrier, including archive, roster, pending join and outbox. Lock/restart drops volatile face keys. Hearth composition grants no extra row principal. Separately verify the place signer only under whichever OPEN JOINT option is ruled (`vault-carriers.ts:239-398`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-487`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:178:# ''Herm / crossroads:'' zero face rows and zero current serving rows; public card/relay service works before any face. No private seed leaks to output. Reboot drops raised ephemeral caps. The place signer's boot behavior follows the OPEN JOINT ruling, not an assumed F classification (`vault-carriers.ts:12-14,240-256`; `stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:179:# ''CLI:'' each run proves its own face; A's prior node admission cannot authorize a B run. No credential/key bytes enter receipts or logs (`stable-founding-road.mem:201`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-180-# ''MCP / watcher:'' each invocation/session proves its face; restarting client transport does not inherit A or authorize B. B succeeds only after B admission (`stable-founding-road.mem:201`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-181-# ''Container node, including harbor/civic/mesh scenarios:'' restart unattended with persistent storage. Public F service starts; all D and any door-gated S refuse until explicit authorized unlock. No image, environment, health endpoint or restart policy supplies a credential. Place signer follows the unresolved option (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
--
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-190-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-191-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:192:''K15.0, this gate:'' freeze the exhaustive node/browser row census, prove each split and owner selector, define FCK/tree/recovery and carrier context, specify old-shape refusal and the isolation red. No identity or custody bytes move. ''K15.1:'' use the existing two-hive same-process isolation probe, then give each face its own store, registry, archive and key seat. ''K15.2:'' make the face the sole identity/group owner; capture two-face event bytes and reject any shared creator/issuer. ''K15.3:'' route sessions and registries by exactly one face and land ''F6 slice three'' asker sealing with the correct row-class key and occupied-row proof. ''K15.4:'' separate content-key rolls and removal by face, including K12 fresh group behavior. ''K15.5:'' one-face restore and a herm that carries without face authoring. ''K15.6:'' one migration fence for identity bytes, split stores, FCK slot trees, archives and custody formats; refuse old mixed archives by name. This follows `stable-founding-road.mem:459,507`. ''B1'' consumes browser no-S key, per-tab face asks, slot-before-first-secret and last-tab floor (`:199-202,206-207`). F6 routing does not precede the key boundary.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-193-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-194-<<~/ahu>>
--
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-198-!!! The floor index of faces (ruled 2026-10-23)
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-199-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:200:A node with no omnibus key still has to name the faces it can unlock before any face opens, so the identity home keeps ONE F row, `face-index`, in the clear. It lists the node's faces in local face order. Each entry holds the SHA-256 digest of its PersonaGroup (null while the founding stands), the digest of the founding's ask (null once the group stands) and the filename of the face's own slot tree. It holds no name, no handle, no prefix, no label and no key bytes.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-201-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-202-''The accepted leak, and only it:'' the count of the node's faces, tombstones included (so a removal history leaks as a count); each PersonaGroup's digest; each founding ask's digest; and each tree's random filename. Nothing else: no name, no handle, no prefix, no label, no key byte, and nothing derived from the face custody id beyond the filename itself. The digests already name the carriers on disk (`face-atom-g<digest>.bin`, `veil-archive-g<digest>.bin`), so the index shows a stolen disk no fact it does not already show. A removed face leaves a tombstone entry, so no other face's local ordinal moves.
--
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-206-''The owner in every D context is the face custody id (`fcid`),'' a random 16-byte id minted with the face's slot tree at the founding's start. It is immutable and never reused. Rows that exist before the PersonaGroup does (the persona root, the founding's atom ask) seal under it, and so do the rows after, so nothing re-seals when the group arrives. The anchors row binds the fcid to the PersonaGroup. Rows are local and never cross vessels, so the id needs no cross-vessel stability.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-207-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:208:The reds: the index of two founded faces carries no field beyond the entries' tree filename, group digest and ask digest and no byte of either face's name, handle, KEL prefix, root key or label, and reads the same whether the faces are locked or open; swapping two entries' trees makes an unlock open a tree and then refuse at the anchors check, with no face seated. The controls: the digests in the index are the digests the carriers on disk bear, the honest index seats its face, and `vault status` prints the count and the digests alone.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-209-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-210-<<~/ahu>>
--
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-223-; The vessel's Keyhive archive (`keyhive-archive`)
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-224-: ''Owner: the place, not a face.'' The archive is minted at the place card (`ensurePlaceCard`, `init`) from the vessel's own seed before any face stands; a faceless or joinee place holds one. It carries the place Individual's membership and prekey secrets and its contact card. It is a NODE-ONLY secret and enters S as the separately named row `place-archive`. Each face's own archive already rests per PersonaGroup as `veil-archive-g<digest>` (D, ''face-hot'').
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:225:: ''No identity above the faces.'' The place Individual signs no face's act and holds no face's key; the daemon island restores each face's own veil from that face's FCK row.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-226-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-227-<<~/ahu>>
--
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-233-* ''The veil leaves the seed.'' `deriveDyadVeil(vesselSeed, groupTag)` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/dyad.ts`) and `groupSecretOpenerFromSeed` (`packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts`) make the vessel seed an ancestor of every face's device veil and group secret. Each becomes D: the veil mints at random at the join and rests under that face's FCK; the opener reads the face's own key. The isolation red gains the row: a thief holding the place seed derives no veil and opens no group secret of any face.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-234-* ''The veil stays with its atom.'' A face's veil rests under that face's FCK on that vessel and rides no one-face bundle; a restore onto a fresh vessel mints a fresh atom and never resurrects the dead one (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/identity-enacts-relation">> #/identity-as-bond, Law 3). The one-face bundle's never-carries list gains the veil.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:235:* ''The census sorts by capability, never by host'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Each present use of a vessel seed, on node and browser alike, sorts by the capability it serves: a floor duty goes to the place signer, which a vessel holds only while it stands a floor; an act on a face's behalf (a dial, a join, an enrolment, a sibling channel, a daemon proof) goes to that face's atom; nothing else keeps a device-wide key. The browser's device seed (`open-browser-vessel.ts`: the Keyhive Individual, the oracle proof, daemon auth, sibling enrolment, the group-secret opener) and the node's (`open-node-vessel.ts`) take the same sort.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:236:* ''The seed's other reach narrows to an allowlist.'' The online place signer serves the floor duties the roadmap's place-signer ruling enumerates; every other present use (the daemon's auth, the device-key signer, the internal asks) either names its domain on that allowlist or takes a key of its own.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-237-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-238-<<~ ahu #/open-joints/the-node-tree-and-the-unlock>>
--
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-242-''The node (S) tree'' is the slot tree `s-slots.bin` wrapping one random S key. It seals exactly two rows: `gate-root` (`gate-root.bin`) and the place archive (`place-archive.bin`). No D row, no face key and no serving key rests under it. The seal frame names it `lares-s-seal`.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-243-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:244:''The tree bytes'' (S, serving and face trees alike, one keyslot shape): a plain JSON tree carries `keyCheck` (the keyed check of the one key its slots wrap) and `slots`, each slot `id`, `t`, `pins` and `wrappedKey`; the check derives under the domain `tree-key-check` and each slot wrap under `slot-wrap`; an S-sealed carrier opens with the magic `lares-s-seal`. These names land before the founding bake, so no earlier spelling is read anywhere.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-245-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-246-''The serving tree'' (`sk-slots.bin`) stands on its own: the serving key (SK) wraps under its own slots, bound over the founding route with a wrapped key, salt and slot ids of their own, and no other key wraps it. A rotation of the S key moves no byte of it.
--
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-250-''One typed credential per unlock.'' `lares vault unlock` opens S and the face this vessel wears; `--face N` names the face; `--all` tries the credential on every face tree. A vessel holding several faces and told none refuses `face-not-named`. The credential reaches only the trees it fits; a face it does not fit stays shut.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-251-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:252:''The sovereign gate.'' The daemon composes sovereign when S stands held AND the worn face stands unlocked (a place with no face needs S alone); otherwise the floor composes. `sovereignStands()` reads this as a pure query. `lares vault lock --face N` drops one face; locking the worn face, or locking S, stands the vessel back at the floor through the same gate, and a sibling's lock leaves sovereign standing. The founding sweep of seeds runs under the gate only.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-253-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-254-''The first founding asks, with nothing preselected:'' whether persona zero's face tree takes the same typed route as the node tree ("one credential then opens both"), answered each time with no default. Either answer leaves the two trees independent.
--
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-261-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-262-
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:263:# ''Node private place signing seed'' (ruled 2026-10-19: a scoped online signer under a pre-rotated place KEL whose next key rides the founding card, built in the founding bake; the options it settled follow). Current `.vessel-key*.json` contains a private signing seed in a floor file and `loadVesselSigningSeed` reads it without a human route (`vault-carriers.ts:240-242`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`). ''Option A:'' keep a *named floor signer* owned by the node, protected at rest under an explicit mechanism; floor means no custody S key/FCK, while this narrowly scoped signer can support faceless Herm startup. Specify the protection, allowed signing/derivation domains, and restart behavior. ''Option B:'' put that signer behind an explicit boot custody door; public card/relay can stand before unlock, while operations requiring its signature wait/refuse until the door opens. Specify Herm boot and unattended container behavior. Neither option is selected here. The private seed is never F, never the current S key, and never the empty serving SK.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:264:# ''Node archive row selector.'' The single `keyhive-archive` carrier is proven (`vault-carriers.ts:290-291`), but the path from each node provider/archive save to the selected face row remains unproved in the handbacks (round 1's YIN handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>; round 4's YANG handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>). Trace every node archive writer and restore reader and show an authenticated PersonaGroup selector before migration. Until then, the D target is a ''LEAD'' and bytes do not move.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-265-# ''Tablet unattended sync.'' Public and ciphertext copy is F. Unattended D decrypt/transform needs a separate product ruling: route it to an authorized always-on node or define a narrowly named S principal, payload and boot door. No background worker receives an FCK by implication (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem-266-# ''Guest-owned store.'' Prove whether the guest persists its own keys/rows. If yes, it is a separate vessel with its own D/F split, FCK trees, floor, and isolation red; if no, every private guest act remains under the host's admitted face session. Host keys never silently become guest keys (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
--
